---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import CalorieCalculator from '@components/islands/CalorieCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';

const calc = getCalculator('health', 'calorie-calculator')!;

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How are my daily calorie needs calculated?',
    answer:
      'The calculator estimates your Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) with the Mifflin-St Jeor equation, then multiplies it by an activity factor to get your Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) — the calories needed to maintain your current weight.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between BMR and TDEE?',
    answer:
      'BMR is the energy your body uses at complete rest just to stay alive. TDEE is BMR multiplied by how active you are, and represents your real daily calorie needs.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How many calories should I cut to lose weight?',
    answer:
      'A deficit of about 500 calories per day corresponds to roughly 0.5 kg (about 1 lb) of weight loss per week. The calculator shows loss and gain targets based on your maintenance level. Avoid very low intakes without professional guidance.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is the Mifflin-St Jeor equation accurate?',
    answer:
      'It is one of the most accurate general equations for estimating BMR in healthy adults, but individual metabolism varies. Use the result as a starting point and adjust based on real-world results.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How many calories should I eat a day?',
    answer:
      'There is no single number — it depends on your size, sex, age and activity. Broad averages often cited are around 2,000 a day for women and 2,500 for men, but your maintenance figure (TDEE) above is far more personal. Base your target on that, then adjust for a loss or gain goal.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How many calories are in a pound of fat?',
    answer:
      'About 3,500 calories. That is why a deficit of roughly 500 calories a day adds up to about a pound of fat loss per week (500 × 7 = 3,500). The rule is an approximation — real results vary — but it explains the loss targets shown here.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  intro="Estimate how many calories you need each day to maintain, lose or gain weight, based on the Mifflin-St Jeor equation and your activity level."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-07-17' }}
>
  <CalorieCalculator />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose your <strong>sex</strong> and <strong>units</strong>.</li>
      <li>Enter your <strong>age</strong>, <strong>height</strong> and <strong>weight</strong>.</li>
      <li>Pick the <strong>activity level</strong> that best matches a typical week.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong> to see your maintenance calories plus gradual loss and gain targets.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How your daily needs are worked out</h2>
    <p>
      The calculator first estimates your Basal Metabolic Rate — the calories you burn at rest —
      then multiplies it by an activity factor to reach your Total Daily Energy Expenditure
      (TDEE). TDEE is your maintenance level: eat that amount and your weight holds steady. The
      loss and gain targets are simply that number with a deficit or surplus applied.
    </p>

    <h2>The Mifflin-St Jeor formula</h2>
    <p>BMR is estimated with the Mifflin-St Jeor equation, then scaled by activity:</p>
    <p><strong>Men: BMR = 10 × kg + 6.25 × cm − 5 × age + 5</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Women: BMR = 10 × kg + 6.25 × cm − 5 × age − 161</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Maintenance (TDEE) = BMR × activity factor</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>kg / cm</strong> — weight in kilograms and height in centimetres (imperial entries are converted for you)</li>
      <li><strong>age</strong> — age in years</li>
      <li><strong>activity factor</strong> — 1.2 for sedentary up to 1.9 for very active</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Choosing your activity level</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sedentary (1.2)</strong> — desk job, little or no exercise.</li>
      <li><strong>Light (1.375)</strong> — light exercise 1–3 days a week.</li>
      <li><strong>Moderate (1.55)</strong> — moderate exercise 3–5 days a week.</li>
      <li><strong>Active (1.725)</strong> — hard exercise 6–7 days a week.</li>
      <li><strong>Very active (1.9)</strong> — very hard exercise or a physical job.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>When in doubt, pick the lower of two levels — most people overestimate how active they are, which inflates the target.</p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Take a <strong>30-year-old woman</strong>, <strong>165 cm</strong> and <strong>68 kg</strong>,
      who exercises a few times a week (moderate, 1.55). Her BMR is 10 × 68 + 6.25 × 165 − 5 × 30
      − 161 ≈ <strong>1,400</strong>, so her maintenance is 1,400 × 1.55 ≈
      <strong>2,170 calories a day</strong>. From there the calculator sets her loss and gain
      targets in 250- and 500-calorie steps:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Goal</th><th>Daily calories</th><th>Approx. weekly change</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Lose faster</td><td>1,670</td><td>−0.5 kg (−1 lb)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lose gradually</td><td>1,920</td><td>−0.25 kg</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Maintain</td><td>2,170</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gain gradually</td><td>2,420</td><td>+0.25 kg</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gain faster</td><td>2,670</td><td>+0.5 kg (+1 lb)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Setting a loss or gain target</h2>
    <p>
      A pound of body fat stores roughly <strong>3,500 calories</strong>, so a deficit of about
      500 a day adds up to about a pound a week (500 × 7 = 3,500). That is why the calculator's
      targets step in 250- and 500-calorie increments. Gentler deficits are usually easier to
      sustain, and a surplus paired with resistance training favours muscle over fat.
    </p>

    <h2>Limitations to keep in mind</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Estimates, not guarantees.</strong> The equation predicts an average; adjust based on how your weight actually responds over a few weeks.</li>
      <li><strong>Activity is self-reported.</strong> The factor is the biggest source of error — an honest, slightly conservative choice serves you better.</li>
      <li><strong>Very low intakes.</strong> Extended, very-low-calorie diets need professional supervision, not a calculator.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      To split your target across macronutrients, see the
      <a href="/health/protein-calculator">protein calculator</a> and
      <a href="/health/fat-intake-calculator">fat intake calculator</a>. These are general
      estimates, not medical or dietary advice; consult a doctor or registered dietitian before
      making significant changes to your diet.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
